#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171714 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172111 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b534245-8276-5e9b-8b9f-0d749116477a" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying the Absolute Value of a Square"}] :question/id #uuid "8b27c37f-10a7-5e55-8230-916bb246142b" :question/math-academy-id "e-1238" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\left| - \\dfrac{x^2}{10} \\right|$." :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}{-a} & = & a\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nwe can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{x^2}{10} \\right|\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication and division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\frac{x^{2}}{10} \\mid &=  \\\\\n\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}x^{2} &}{\\mid 10\\end{vmatrix}} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\mid x^{2} \\mid}{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nx^2 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor any real $x$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left|x^2\\right &= x^2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mid x^{2} \\mid}{10} &= \\frac{x^{2}}{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171715 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172112 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60502703-f918-51c6-98dc-62fdd6e3241e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Absolute Value Expression with a Domain Restriction"}] :question/id #uuid "e0d4030c-4490-5e36-af26-7ded6003e841" :question/math-academy-id "e-1239" :question/problem "Given that $x\\lt 0$, simplify $\\mid {-6x} \\mid +2x$." :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}{-a} & = & a\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nwe can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\mid 6x \\mid +2x\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid 6x \\mid + 2x &=  \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}6 & \\cdot & x\\end{vmatrix}+ 2x &=  \\\\\n6 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}x & + 2x &= \\\\ 6 & x\\end{vmatrix}+ 2x \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $x \\lt 0$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid x &= -x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 \\mid x\\begin{vmatrix}+ 2x &= \\\\ 6(-x) + 2x|= \\\\ -6x + 2x|= \\\\ -4x\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171819 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172015 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72810e1e-8a12-5488-8aac-3a94cc027e68" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Absolute Value Expressions"}] :question/id #uuid "9c4b5b23-3d35-593a-ae69-4766e5b1d996" :question/math-academy-id "e-6800" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\begin{vmatrix}{-3z} & -5\\cdot & 4z\\end{vmatrix}$." :question/worked-solution "Firstly, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}{-a} & = & a\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nwe can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}3z & -5\\cdot & 4z\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}3z & -5 \\cdot & 4z\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}3 & \\cdot & z & -5 \\cdot & 4 & \\cdot & z\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}z & -5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot & z & &= \\\\ 3 \\cdot & z & -20 \\cdot & z\\end{vmatrix}|= \\\\\n3\\begin{vmatrix}z & -20 & z\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n&= - 17 \\mid z \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172015 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72810e1e-8a12-5488-8aac-3a94cc027e68" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Absolute Value Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1aa2fee-c2fb-55e9-ace9-208c5cc6a263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171070} {:answer/id #uuid "0237105b-b02f-5bd1-8e36-ec330b85285d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 \\mid x \\mid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171071} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca8b6ec-2c0b-5bfa-9e33-b41764